# Engineering Claim: Fuel Cell-Battery Power Conditioning Circuit with DC Link Voltage Stabilization

## Claim

A power conditioning circuit for a hybrid fuel cell-battery power system, comprising:

**1. A fuel cell power source** configured to supply a primary DC output voltage, said fuel cell power source having an inherent output impedance characteristic that produces a variable terminal voltage as a function of load current draw;

**2. A battery power source** configured to supply a secondary DC voltage, said battery power source coupled to a bidirectional DC-DC battery converter, the battery converter comprising:
   - a plurality of switching elements arranged in a half-bridge or full-bridge topology;
   - an inductive energy storage element coupled between the battery power source and a switching node;
   - a gate driver circuit configured to generate pulse-width-modulated (PWM) switching signals at a controllable duty cycle;

**3. A DC link bus** electrically coupled to both the fuel cell power source output and the battery converter output, said DC link bus characterized by a DC link voltage that is subject to ripple perturbations induced by the switching operation of the battery converter, said ripple perturbations having a fundamental frequency component substantially equal to the switching frequency of the battery converter and harmonic components thereof;

**4. A DC link voltage sensing circuit** configured to sample the DC link voltage at a sampling rate sufficient to resolve said ripple perturbations, and to generate a sensed voltage signal representative thereof;

**5. A voltage regulation controller**, communicatively coupled to the DC link voltage sensing circuit and to the gate driver circuit of the battery converter, said voltage regulation controller comprising:
   - a reference voltage generator configured to establish a target DC link voltage setpoint;
   - an error signal generator configured to compute a deviation between the sensed voltage signal and the target DC link voltage setpoint;
   - a compensator, comprising at least a proportional-integral (PI) or proportional-integral-derivative (PID) control stage, configured to process said deviation and generate a duty cycle correction signal;
   - a ripple suppression filter, comprising a notch filter or low-pass filter tuned substantially to the switching frequency of the battery converter, said ripple suppression filter interposed in the feedback path between the DC link voltage sensing circuit and the error signal generator, configured to attenuate the ripple perturbation components from the sensed voltage signal prior to error computation, thereby preventing said ripple perturbations from being erroneously interpreted as steady-state voltage deviations;

**6. A PWM modulator**, coupled to the output of the voltage regulation controller, configured to convert the duty cycle correction signal into gate drive timing signals for the switching elements of the battery converter, thereby adjusting the effective duty cycle of the battery converter to regulate power flow between the battery power source and the DC link bus;

**wherein** the voltage regulation controller is configured to dynamically adjust the duty cycle of the battery converter in a closed-loop manner such that the DC link voltage is maintained within a predetermined tolerance band of the target DC link voltage setpoint despite variations in fuel cell output voltage, load current transients, and battery converter switching-induced ripple;

**and wherein** the ripple suppression filter cooperates with the compensator to achieve a control bandwidth that is lower than the switching frequency of the battery converter by at least one order of magnitude, thereby ensuring stability of the closed-loop voltage regulation while suppressing propagation of switching-frequency ripple onto the DC link bus and onto any auxiliary loads coupled thereto.

---

### Optional Dependent Claim Elements

- **wherein** the fuel cell power source further comprises a fuel cell DC-DC boost converter interposed between the fuel cell and the DC link bus, said boost converter operating in a voltage-source mode to establish a baseline DC link voltage, while the battery converter operates in a current-source or power-following mode to manage transient load demands and state-of-charge regulation of the battery;

- **wherein** the voltage regulation controller further comprises a feedforward compensation path responsive to a measured load current or fuel cell output current, configured to preemptively adjust the duty cycle correction signal in anticipation of load transients, thereby reducing transient voltage excursions on the DC link bus;

- **wherein** the DC link voltage sensing circuit comprises an anti-aliasing filter having a cutoff frequency selected to be below the Nyquist frequency of an analog-to-digital converter sampling the DC link voltage, said anti-aliasing filter cooperating with the ripple suppression filter to further reduce measurement noise attributable to battery converter switching;

- **wherein** the compensator gains of the voltage regulation controller are adaptively adjusted based on a detected state-of-charge of the battery power source or a detected operating point of the fuel cell power source, so as to maintain consistent DC link voltage regulation performance across varying source impedance conditions.
